---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const base = '/prisma-to-sql';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="$batch & @optimize - prisma-sql"
  description="Combine independent reads into one round trip with $batch and prebake hot queries at generate time with @optimize."
  active={`${base}/docs/batch-optimize`}
>
  <h1>$batch and @optimize</h1>
  <p>Extras on top of Prisma's API: capabilities Prisma Client does not have, with the same types and code style.</p>

  <h2 id="batch">$batch</h2>
  <p><code>$batch</code> runs multiple independent read queries in one PostgreSQL round trip.</p>
  <pre><code>{`const dashboard = await prisma.$batch((batch) => ({
  totalUsers: batch.user.count(),
  activeUsers: batch.user.count({
    where: { status: 'ACTIVE' },
  }),
  recentProjects: batch.project.findMany({
    take: 5,
    orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },
    include: { organization: true },
  }),
  taskStats: batch.task.aggregate({
    _count: true,
    _avg: { estimatedHours: true },
    where: { status: 'IN_PROGRESS' },
  }),
}))`}</code></pre>

  <h3>Rules</h3>
  <p>Do not <code>await</code> inside the callback.</p>
  <pre><code>{`// Incorrect
await prisma.$batch(async (batch) => ({
  users: await batch.user.findMany(),
}))

// Correct
await prisma.$batch((batch) => ({
  users: batch.user.findMany(),
}))`}</code></pre>

  <h3>Best use cases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>dashboards</li>
    <li>analytics summaries</li>
    <li>counts + page data</li>
    <li>multiple independent aggregates</li>
    <li>splitting unrelated heavy reads instead of one massive include tree</li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    Limitations: PostgreSQL only. Queries are independent and not transactional. Use <code>$transaction</code> when you
    need transactional guarantees.
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="optimize">@optimize</h2>
  <p>
    For the hottest query shapes you can prebake SQL at generate time. Matching runtime shapes execute the baked SQL
    directly; non-matching shapes use normal runtime generation.
  </p>

  <h3>Basic prebaked query</h3>
  <pre><code>{`/// @optimize {
///   "method": "findMany",
///   "query": {
///     "where": { "status": "ACTIVE" }
///   }
/// }
model User {
  id     Int    @id
  status String
}`}</code></pre>

  <h3>Dynamic parameters</h3>
  <p>Values prefixed with <code>$</code> become bind parameters at runtime.</p>
  <pre><code>{`/// @optimize {
///   "method": "findMany",
///   "query": {
///     "where": { "status": "$status" },
///     "skip": "$skip",
///     "take": "$take"
///   }
/// }`}</code></pre>

  <h3>Nested include</h3>
  <pre><code>{`/// @optimize {
///   "method": "findMany",
///   "query": {
///     "include": {
///       "posts": {
///         "where": { "published": true },
///         "orderBy": { "createdAt": "desc" },
///         "take": 5
///       }
///     }
///   }
/// }`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>@optimize</code> is optional — it only removes the conversion overhead of repeated hot query shapes. See
    <a href={`${base}/docs/configuration`}>generator configuration</a> for the <code>skipInvalid</code> option if a
    directive should be skipped instead of failing generation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do you need <code>@optimize</code>? No. Runtime generation already covers every supported shape; prebaking is a
    micro-optimization for your hottest endpoints.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
